// O(1) index-linked free-slot allocator (GitHub issue #207). Replaces the
// repeated `for i in 0..<MAX { if used[i] == 0 ... }` linear scan that used
// to appear independently in kernel/mm/page.tkb, kernel/kernel/process.tkb,
// kernel/mm/address_space.tkb, and kernel/net/tcp.tkb with O(1) alloc/free,
// while preserving each site's existing "stable owner slot + generation
// counter" ABA-safety discipline (see SPEC.md's "Indexed Runtime Owners").
//
// Ported from linux_user/freelist_generic/freelist.tkb (issue #207's design
// prototype -- see that file's own header for the two concrete compiler
// facts that shaped the FreelistCore(N)/Freelist(T, N) split) with one
// addition needed for kernel/ call sites that expose their OWN owner type
// (PageOwner[page], ScheduledProcessOwner[process], etc.) rather than this
// library's FreelistOwner directly: freelist_owner_into_parts() consumes a
// FreelistOwner and returns its raw (index, generation) without releasing
// the slot, so a caller can immediately rewrap them in its own owner type.
// This mirrors page.tkb's own pre-existing page_owner_into_parts/
// page_owner_from_parts round trip, which predates this library and is left
// untouched -- callers reconstruct a fresh FreelistOwner via
// freelist_owner_new() to give back to freelist_core_remove() on free,
// which does not itself validate the generation (only affine "consumed
// exactly once" discipline is enforced there), so this round trip weakens
// nothing.
//
// Not adopted by every linear-scan call site GitHub issue #207 lists:
// kernel/kernel/fd_table.tkb's shared object pool uses a plain
// (non-linear) reference-counted UnifiedObjectHandle, not a linear owner --
// a fundamentally different resource-lifetime model this library's affine
// FreelistOwner does not fit -- and several of its scans search for a slot
// matching a key, not a free slot, which no free-list design addresses.
// kernel/net/tcp.tkb's PENDING_TCP_MAX retry queue has no owner/generation
// concept at all (a plain fixed retry-slot array). Both are left as
// candidates for a follow-up issue rather than forced into this shape
// (see issue #242 for the fd_table.tkb case specifically).
//
// Current limitations (2026-08-07). Keep this list current as call sites
// change; a limitation that goes stale here is worse than none, since it
// invites relying on a guarantee that quietly stopped holding.
// - NOT SYNCHRONIZED. FreelistCore(N)/its callers (Freelist(T, N),
//   kernel/lib/slotmap.tkb's SlotMap(N)) do no locking of their own.
//   Every current kernel/ instance (kernel/mm/page.tkb, kernel/kernel/
//   process.tkb, kernel/mm/address_space.tkb, kernel/net/tcp.tkb's
//   connection pool) is, as of this writing, reachable ONLY from
//   syscall-dispatch context on core 0 -- never from an `!{interrupt}`
//   handler, never from core 1 (which only proves PSCI/EL1 bring-up and
//   parks, touching no shared kernel state). That is a fact about today's
//   callers, not a property this file enforces or checks. The failure
//   mode this guards against: a library that works by accident because
//   every caller so far happened to be single-context gets adopted by a
//   caller that isn't, and starts corrupting the free chain under
//   concurrent access with no compiler diagnostic pointing at why --
//   exactly the shape of bug issue #207 already found once (two
//   sequential, single-threaded double frees silently corrupting the
//   chain; see HISTORY.md). A genuinely concurrent caller (a future
//   interrupt handler or core 2 user, see issue #242's design
//   discussion) needs the SAME kind of hand-off this codebase already
//   uses for cross-context shared state elsewhere: put the pool (or the
//   specific field a concurrent caller touches) behind a lock/guard pair
//   and stable_replace, the pattern kernel/kernel/process.tkb's
//   ScheduledProcessStore, kernel/net/tcp.tkb's TcpConnectionStore, and
//   kernel/mm/process_image.tkb's ProcessCloneVmStore already establish
//   -- not a change to this file itself.
// - Capacity N is fixed at compile time (a const-generic parameter), not
//   grown or shrunk at runtime.
// - freelist_core_init()/slotmap_init() must run before first use (see
//   freelist_core_init's own comment) -- a pool's backing memory being
//   zero-initialized .bss is NOT equivalent to an initialized empty pool,
//   unlike the linear-scan allocators this replaced.
// - The explicit probe begin/commit pair is `unsafe` and exists only so the
//   two-core contention fixture can pause after reading free_head. A pending
//   read must be committed against the same FreelistCore that produced it or
//   cancelled; ordinary callers use freelist_core_insert, which performs the
//   same private begin/commit immediately.

use "kernel/lib/execution_model.tkb";

must_use variant FreelistPopResult {
    Empty;
    Popped(exists allocation: usize. FreelistOwner[allocation]);
}

// A head read that has not yet been committed. Normal callers never see
// this split: freelist_core_insert begins and commits immediately. The
// contention probe uses the explicit unsafe pair to rendezvous two cores
// after both have read the head, making the primitive's read/update race
// reproducible under host scheduling that otherwise runs one vCPU through
// the whole operation.
linear struct FreelistPopPending[pending: usize] {
    private head: usize @ pending;
}

must_use variant FreelistPopProbeBeginResult {
    Empty;
    Pending(exists pending: usize. FreelistPopPending[pending]);
}

private fn freelist_pop_pending_new(head: usize @ pending)
        -> FreelistPopPending[pending] {
    let mut value: FreelistPopPending[pending] = { head };
    return value;
}

private linear struct FreelistOwner[allocation: usize] {
    private index: usize;
    private generation: usize @ allocation;
}

fn freelist_owner_new(index: usize, generation: usize @ allocation)
        -> FreelistOwner[allocation] {
    let mut owner: FreelistOwner[allocation] = { index, generation };
    return owner;
}

fn freelist_owner_index(owner: borrow FreelistOwner[allocation]) -> usize {
    return owner.index;
}

fn freelist_owner_generation(owner: borrow FreelistOwner[allocation]) -> usize {
    return owner.generation;
}

// Consumes the owner without returning its slot to the pool, for a caller
// that immediately rewraps (index, generation) in its own owner type (see
// this file's header comment). The paired reconstruction is
// freelist_owner_new(index, generation) -- there is deliberately no single
// "convert in place" primitive, since the two callers of each half (this
// library's Popped case vs. a site's own xxx_free()) are never the same
// call site.
fn freelist_owner_into_parts(owner: sink FreelistOwner[allocation])
        -> (usize, usize @ allocation) {
    return (owner.index, owner.generation);
}

// Consumes the owner WITHOUT returning its slot to the pool. Only for a
// caller that has already established the slot must not be pushed (see
// kernel/lib/slotmap.tkb's double-free detection); the linear obligation
// still has to be discharged, which is exactly what this does.
fn freelist_owner_drop(owner: sink FreelistOwner[allocation]) {}

// N is this pool's capacity, embedded directly -- no caller-supplied
// backing array for the bookkeeping index at all.
generic struct FreelistCore(N: usize) {
    next_free: [usize; N];
    free_head: usize;
    next_generation: usize;
}

fn freelist_core_init(core: &mut FreelistCore(N)) {
    // GitHub issue #217: core.next_free[i] indexes the struct-embedded
    // array field directly, through the same checked TArray/
    // emit_bounds_check codegen path a local array's own indexing
    // already gets -- no more manual `let`/raw-*usize-pointer-arithmetic
    // workaround (issue #15) needed here. The for-loop counter's own
    // {0..<N} refined type proves this in range at compile time, so
    // there is no runtime trap site left to elide even at -O0.
    for i: usize in 0..<N {
        core.next_free[i] = i + 1;
    }
    core.free_head = 0;
    core.next_generation = 1;
}

// Initialise with no allocatable entries. A caller whose storage is
// discovered at runtime can then add exactly the indices backed by that
// storage instead of briefly publishing every compile-time metadata slot.
fn freelist_core_init_empty(core: &mut FreelistCore(N)) {
    core.free_head = N;
    core.next_generation = 1;
}

fn freelist_core_add_index(core: &mut FreelistCore(N),
                           index: usize) {
    if (index < N) {
        core.next_free[index] = core.free_head;
        core.free_head = index;
    }
}

private fn freelist_core_insert_begin(core: &mut FreelistCore(N))
        -> FreelistPopProbeBeginResult {
    let head: usize = core.free_head;
    if (head >= 0 && head < N) {
        return FreelistPopProbeBeginResult::Pending(
            freelist_pop_pending_new(head));
    }
    return FreelistPopProbeBeginResult::Empty;
}

private fn freelist_core_insert_commit(
        core: &mut FreelistCore(N),
        pending: sink FreelistPopPending[pending_head])
        -> FreelistPopResult !{allocates} {
    let head: usize = pending.head;
    if (head < N) {
        // GitHub issue #217: the begin step only produces Pending after
        // narrowing head to the array bound. Recheck at this function
        // boundary so the proof remains local and --forbid-trap needs no
        // exception.
        core.free_head = core.next_free[head];
        let generation: usize = core.next_generation;
        core.next_generation = generation + 1;
        return FreelistPopResult::Popped(freelist_owner_new(head, generation));
    }
    return FreelistPopResult::Empty;
}

fn freelist_core_insert(core: &mut FreelistCore(N)) -> FreelistPopResult
        !{allocates} {
    match freelist_core_insert_begin(core) {
        FreelistPopProbeBeginResult::Empty => {
            return FreelistPopResult::Empty;
        }
        FreelistPopProbeBeginResult::Pending(pending) => {
            return freelist_core_insert_commit(core, pending);
        }
    }
}

// Unsafe because holding a pending head read across another core's mutation
// is deliberately the operation whose race the kernel contention probe
// demonstrates. The linear pending value still makes commit or cancel
// mandatory on every path. The type does not carry the originating core;
// committing it against any other core is invalid.
fn freelist_core_insert_probe_begin(core: &mut FreelistCore(N))
        -> FreelistPopProbeBeginResult !{unsafe} {
    return freelist_core_insert_begin(core);
}

fn freelist_core_insert_probe_commit(
        core: &mut FreelistCore(N),
        pending: sink FreelistPopPending[pending_head])
        -> FreelistPopResult !{allocates, unsafe} {
    return freelist_core_insert_commit(core, pending);
}

fn freelist_core_insert_probe_cancel(
        pending: sink FreelistPopPending[pending_head]) !{unsafe} {}

// GitHub issue #380: take every free entry in [first, first + count) OFF
// the list, in ONE pass over it.
//
// A contiguous allocator has to choose its pages by POSITION, and this
// list is singly linked -- it can pop the head and push a node back in
// O(1) and has no way to remove a specific node. Rebuilding that ability
// per page would be O(N) per page; doing it for a whole run at once is
// O(N) for the run, which is what makes a run allocation affordable.
//
// Returns how many entries were unlinked, so the caller can check its own
// arithmetic against the list's answer rather than trusting the scan that
// chose the range.
fn freelist_core_unlink_range(core: &mut FreelistCore(N), first: usize,
                              count: usize) -> usize !{unsafe} {
    let mut unlinked: usize = 0;
    // Entries at the HEAD first, so the walk below always has a real
    // predecessor to rewrite.
    let mut walking: bool = true;
    let mut head_steps: usize = 0;
    while (walking && head_steps <= N) {
        head_steps = head_steps + 1;
        let head: usize = core.free_head;
        if (head < N && head >= first && head - first < count) {
            core.free_head = core.next_free[head];
            unlinked = unlinked + 1;
        } else {
            walking = false;
        }
    }
    // Bounded, not trusting termination: a corrupted `next_free` can make
    // this list cyclic, and an unbounded walk over one hangs the kernel
    // with no diagnosis. kernel/lib/intrusive_pool.tkb bounds every chain
    // walk it does for the same reason (issue #355's invariant probe says
    // so explicitly); this one was written without that and should not
    // have been. N steps is a full traversal of the longest possible
    // list, so reaching it means the list is not a list.
    let mut prev: usize = core.free_head;
    let mut steps: usize = 0;
    while (prev < N && steps <= N) {
        steps = steps + 1;
        let next: usize = unsafe { core.next_free[prev] };
        if (next < N && next >= first && next - first < count) {
            unsafe { core.next_free[prev] = core.next_free[next] };
            unlinked = unlinked + 1;
        } else {
            prev = next;
        }
    }
    return unlinked;
}

fn freelist_core_remove(core: &mut FreelistCore(N), owner: sink FreelistOwner[allocation]) !{unsafe} {
    // GitHub issue #216: owner.index is only ever produced by
    // freelist_core_insert from this same core's next_free, so it is
    // always in range in practice, but that fact crosses a function-call
    // boundary -- FreelistOwner's own `index` field is a plain usize
    // with no compile-time-visible bound (see #216 for the "extend it
    // with a second erased static value parameter tracking the pool's
    // capacity" idea, deliberately not committed to here; #200/#201's
    // research track is the eventual real fix). GitHub issue #217 closed
    // the companion gap that used to hide this call entirely: indexing
    // core.next_free is now a REAL checked array access, so this one
    // line needs an explicit `unsafe` marker to skip the bounds check.
    // That marker is the actual improvement over the old state -- this
    // site is grep-visible and --forbid-trap-exempt only THROUGH it, not
    // silently invisible to the checker the way the old *usize-decay
    // pointer-arithmetic workaround left it.
    unsafe { core.next_free[freelist_owner_index(owner)] = core.free_head };
    core.free_head = freelist_owner_index(owner);
}

generic struct Freelist(T: type, N: usize) {
    core: FreelistCore(N);
    data: [T; N..];
}

fn freelist_init(T: type, target: &mut Freelist(T, N), data_backing: [T; N..]) {
    freelist_core_init(&target.core);
    target.data = data_backing;
}

fn freelist_insert(T: type, target: &mut Freelist(T, N)) -> FreelistPopResult {
    return freelist_core_insert(&target.core);
}

fn freelist_remove(T: type, target: &mut Freelist(T, N),
                    owner: sink FreelistOwner[allocation]) {
    freelist_core_remove(&target.core, owner);
}

// Returns a pointer to T tied to owner's slot -- no copy, whatever T's
// size. Field access through the result needs no explicit deref (e.g.
// `freelist_ref(&target, owner).x = 1;`); a whole-T read/write does
// (`*freelist_ref(&target, owner) = v;`), which is the expensive case
// made to visibly look expensive, exactly as intended when this shape
// was designed. `data` is a genuine slice field here (no decay), so the
// `as *T` cast is needed to get a raw pointer for the offset -- this
// whole function is raw pointer arithmetic (issue #15), same as it has
// been since the very first #207 worked example.
fn freelist_ref(T: type, target: &mut Freelist(T, N),
                owner: borrow FreelistOwner[allocation]) -> *T {
    let data = target.data;
    let index: isize = freelist_owner_index(owner) as isize;
    return (data as *T) + index;
}

// GitHub issue #479: this file's execution-model assertion is GONE, and
// the reason is not that the race went away -- it is that the claim
// stopped being about the core count.
//
// It used to say "the free chain has no lock, and a concurrent push
// corrupts it silently", gated on KERNEL_ACTIVE_CORES == 1. That was
// measured: two cores cycling one FreelistCore(8) were handed the same
// index 1349 times on RPi5 (3 under QEMU), and
// kernel/kernel/freelist_contention_evidence.tkb produces it on demand.
//
// But the fix was not a lock HERE. The one live kernel instance is
// kernel/mm/page.tkb's boot_page_pool.core, and page_alloc pops from it and
// then writes three fields of meta[index] -- a sequence a lock inside the
// pop could not cover. page.tkb holds a Mutex across the whole of it.
//
// So this primitive is unlocked BY DESIGN, at any core count, and its
// requirement is on the CALLER. A static_assert on the core count would
// fire on a build where nothing is wrong, which sends the next reader
// looking for a defect that is not there. What holds the requirement up
// instead is the probe above, which demonstrates both halves every boot:
// unlocked hands one index to two cores, locked does not.
